1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Meet Livia — home rehabilitation made visible.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Patients exercise between appointments, but physiotherapists rarely see
what happens during those sessions.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,000
With Livia, the physiotherapist sets the exercises and targets.
The patient simply starts the prescribed session.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:32,500
First, shoulder abduction. The pose model tracks the body on each frame,
while Livia's rule-based exercise policy follows the arm through the movement.

5
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:45,500
It counts completed repetitions and gives specific feedback when movement is
rushed or the range is incomplete. It detected all nine reps in this recording.

6
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:59,500
The second prototype is sit-to-stand, filmed from the side. Livia chooses the
most visible body side and follows the shoulder, hip, knee and ankle.

7
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:15,100
Six repetitions were completed. The first five took 22.2 seconds.
This prototype exercise policy has not been clinically validated.

8
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:22,600
At the end, the patient sees completed reps, timing
and whether tracking was interrupted.

9
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:30,100
They can add pain, exertion and a note. The product is designed to retain
useful session measurements instead of raw video.

10
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:37,600
The physiotherapist gets a clear pre-visit report:
adherence first, followed by movement details to review.

11
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:45,100
Patient context comes next. Livia does not diagnose or change the plan.
The physiotherapist remains in control.

12
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:53,100
Five physiotherapists saw value in this visibility.
Next, we want to test Livia with a clinical design partner.
